No Docker needed — Azure has a way to do this entirely server-side. `az acr import` copies an image directly from one registry to another (including from a public registry into yours), without ever touching your local machine.

## The fix — no Docker required

```bash
az acr import --name stageb5t3acr --source mcr.microsoft.com/azuredocs/containerapps-helloworld:latest --image backend:placeholder
```

This tells Azure: "go fetch this public image and copy it directly into `stageb5t3acr`, server to server." Takes a few seconds, no local pull/push needed.

**Verify it landed:**
```bash
az acr repository show --name stageb5t3acr --repository backend
```

## Then update `main.tf`

Open `main.tf`, find the `module "container_apps"` block, and change:
```hcl
backend_image  = "mcr.microsoft.com/azuredocs/containerapps-helloworld:latest"
frontend_image = "mcr.microsoft.com/azuredocs/containerapps-helloworld:latest"
```

to:
```hcl
backend_image  = "stageb5t3acr.azurecr.io/backend:placeholder"
frontend_image = "stageb5t3acr.azurecr.io/backend:placeholder"
```

(Reusing the same image/tag for both is fine — it's just a placeholder to prove the infra works; both will just run the generic hello-world container for now.)

## Re-apply

```bash
terraform plan 2>&1 | grep -i "mcr.microsoft\|stageb5t3acr.azurecr"
```
Confirm the new image reference shows up correctly, then:
```bash
terraform apply -target=module.container_apps
```

**One thing to watch this time:** since a previous failed attempt already created (or partially created) `stage-backend`, this apply might need to **update** the existing container app rather than create fresh — that's fine and expected, Terraform will show `~` (update in place) instead of `+`. Just don't be alarmed if it's not a plain "create."

Run the `az acr import` command first and confirm it succeeds before touching `main.tf`.
